\subsection{Break-even Kernel Startup Time}
We can see that the asynchronous kernel startup time stays roughly constant until a processing time of around 3100 clock cycles, after which it starts rising linearly with increasing clock cycles. This indicates that until 3100 cycles the the GPU spends time idle even after the kernel is finish processing. After 3100 cycles the next kernel can be directly launched without additional overhead. Roughly 8200 clock cycles are needed, until the initial asynchronous time of around 2.7s doubles. This time of around 5.3s consists of 2.7s for the kernel startup time in addition to another 2.7s coming from time only spent processing.